            <div xml:id="d744">

                <pb facs="0989" n="889" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg889"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">691.116 Auto/62: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Persia</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornibrook</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-12-20T17:00:00-05:00">December 20, 1934—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>46. Your 63, December 18, 2 p.m., and 64, December 19, 2 p.m.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d744-f1"/> The Department assumes that the Persian Government
                    realizes that, as set <pb facs="0990" n="890" xml:id="pg890"/> forth in the
                    Department’s telegram No. 35 of November 8, 6 p.m., any monopoly which
                    discriminates between American automotive interests would probably render
                    impracticable the proposed negotiations for a reciprocal trade agreement. If you
                    have any reason to believe that the Persian authorities are not fully aware of
                    this fact, you are authorized in your discretion again to bring it to their
                    attention.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
